WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.657 --> 00:00:02.422
- [Kelly] I was just a goofball
as a kid, so I actually

00:00:02.422 --> 00:00:04.466
wanted to be a comedic actor.

00:00:04.466 --> 00:00:06.786
And I think that was one thing
my mom always wanted for me,

00:00:06.786 --> 00:00:08.369
she always wanted me to follow my dreams.

00:00:08.369 --> 00:00:10.687
Has anyone in here ever wanted
to get worse at something?

00:00:10.687 --> 00:00:13.691
I think probably in that
moment I knew that this is what

00:00:13.691 --> 00:00:15.490
I was going to do forever.

00:00:15.490 --> 00:00:17.463
You know, the real
inspiration comes from just

00:00:17.463 --> 00:00:19.152
the pleasure of doing things.

00:00:19.152 --> 00:00:21.655
You know, if you get too
focused on competition sometimes

00:00:21.655 --> 00:00:23.214
and you don't have
anything else happening,

00:00:23.214 --> 00:00:25.217
then you get lopsided.

00:00:25.217 --> 00:00:28.198
I had many, many years where
I had all my energy strictly

00:00:28.198 --> 00:00:30.238
going in to trying to improve
my surfing, win heats,

00:00:30.238 --> 00:00:32.442
and that was my laser focus.

00:00:32.442 --> 00:00:34.063
You know, if you're not getting better,

00:00:34.063 --> 00:00:38.559
then it's not fun
anymore, that's my theory.

00:00:38.559 --> 00:00:42.628
I guess a measure of success
is, you know, the end result.

00:00:42.628 --> 00:00:43.891
I'm not a religious person.

00:00:43.891 --> 00:00:46.535
I don't even know if I believe in God.

00:00:46.535 --> 00:00:49.324
But it was a gift from
God, it was like this pshh.

00:00:49.324 --> 00:00:51.286
- He's an American
professional surfer known

00:00:51.286 --> 00:00:53.325
for his competitiveness and style.

00:00:53.325 --> 00:00:55.532
He's been crowned World
Surf League Champion

00:00:55.532 --> 00:00:57.361
a record 11 times.

00:00:57.361 --> 00:01:01.498
In 2007 he became the all time
leader in career event wins.

00:01:01.498 --> 00:01:03.302
He's Kelly Slater, and here are his

00:01:03.302 --> 00:01:05.039
top 10 rules for success.

00:01:05.039 --> 00:01:07.372
(whooshing)

00:01:08.333 --> 00:01:12.661
- [Kelly] Surfing in it's most
basic sense is just a true

00:01:12.661 --> 00:01:14.267
freedom of expression.

00:01:14.267 --> 00:01:16.599
I was just a goofball as a
kid, so I actually wanted to be

00:01:16.599 --> 00:01:20.766
a comedic actor up until the
time I was about 10 years old.

00:01:22.264 --> 00:01:25.413
Well, surfing has really defined my life

00:01:25.413 --> 00:01:28.333
on probably every level in some way.

00:01:28.333 --> 00:01:32.551
I believe in certain people,
there's just this built in

00:01:32.551 --> 00:01:34.551
desire and drive to win.

00:01:35.648 --> 00:01:40.429
It's sort of beyond what
other people can understand.

00:01:40.429 --> 00:01:43.346
(electronic music)

00:02:05.648 --> 00:02:09.698
I come from a small city in
Florida, in central Florida.

00:02:09.698 --> 00:02:12.969
And um, there's not too
many people in my city

00:02:12.969 --> 00:02:15.377
who sort of spread out around the world,

00:02:15.377 --> 00:02:17.773
and I live around the world,
I travel around the world,

00:02:17.773 --> 00:02:20.009
and it's really great, it's a lot of fun.

00:02:20.009 --> 00:02:23.568
But, I think as a kid, I
had a lot of ideas that were

00:02:23.568 --> 00:02:27.565
really big, and both
my parents had passions

00:02:27.565 --> 00:02:30.011
that they never really followed.

00:02:30.011 --> 00:02:32.286
Both my parents loved to play music.

00:02:32.286 --> 00:02:36.447
Dad played guitar, my mom
loved to sing and play banjo.

00:02:36.447 --> 00:02:39.553
My dad did a little bit
of theatrical acting.

00:02:39.553 --> 00:02:43.040
But they never really
followed their dreams,

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:44.460
either one of them, you know?

00:02:44.460 --> 00:02:47.028
And I think that was one thing
my mom always wanted for me,

00:02:47.028 --> 00:02:49.576
she always wanted me to follow
my dreams and to think big.

00:02:49.576 --> 00:02:54.053
Don't just think of like,
you know, the little problem

00:02:54.053 --> 00:02:56.112
you have at school, or just
a little, I mean you got to

00:02:56.112 --> 00:02:58.665
take care of those things too,
but it's thinking about the,

00:02:58.665 --> 00:03:02.062
you know, someone like the
Dalai Lama, he's thinking

00:03:02.062 --> 00:03:04.866
about the whole world every
time he make's a decision.

00:03:04.866 --> 00:03:07.288
If more people think that way,

00:03:07.288 --> 00:03:09.705
more big great things happen.

00:03:10.694 --> 00:03:13.611
(electronic music)

00:03:34.952 --> 00:03:38.518
Has anyone in here ever wanted
to get worse at something?

00:03:38.518 --> 00:03:40.480
(crowd laughing)

00:03:40.480 --> 00:03:43.599
I mean, if you're trying to
get better, you're competitive.

00:03:43.599 --> 00:03:45.526
Whether it's with yourself
or with someone else.

00:03:45.526 --> 00:03:48.916
Some people try to get more better.

00:03:48.916 --> 00:03:51.394
Does that make sense? Mo better.

00:03:51.394 --> 00:03:52.611
Hold on that's not what I meant.

00:03:52.611 --> 00:03:53.988
(crowd laughing)

00:03:53.988 --> 00:03:56.736
- Mo betta. Better-er.
- Better-er.

00:03:56.736 --> 00:03:58.180
(crowd laughing)

00:03:58.180 --> 00:04:00.236
- But, Rob and I were both
raised in very competitive homes.

00:04:00.236 --> 00:04:03.736
Rob's dad made the Malloy brothers' father

00:04:05.190 --> 00:04:07.460
break a ping pong paddle and cry one time

00:04:07.460 --> 00:04:08.437
in his own home.

00:04:08.437 --> 00:04:09.832
(crowd laughing)

00:04:09.832 --> 00:04:10.854
This is not a joke.

00:04:10.854 --> 00:04:12.105
- No, it was serious.

00:04:12.105 --> 00:04:14.038
- He actually threw the
paddle and broke the wall,

00:04:14.038 --> 00:04:15.421
screaming, cussing I heard.

00:04:15.421 --> 00:04:16.254
- Yes.

00:04:16.254 --> 00:04:18.603
- But, Rob's dad and his
older brother are like

00:04:18.603 --> 00:04:20.692
crazy, crazy competitive guys.

00:04:20.692 --> 00:04:23.452
Like, that's how I always
knew them, hanging out

00:04:23.452 --> 00:04:25.398
when we were teenagers, you know?

00:04:25.398 --> 00:04:28.182
Like, Jim would beat us at tennis,

00:04:28.182 --> 00:04:29.780
and then he was way
better than us at golf,

00:04:29.780 --> 00:04:31.617
and he would like take
us to the golf range

00:04:31.617 --> 00:04:32.905
and we would be hittin
balls, and he'd be like,

00:04:32.905 --> 00:04:34.760
what the hell are you doing?

00:04:34.760 --> 00:04:35.794
(crowd laughing)

00:04:35.794 --> 00:04:37.440
That looks terrible. That's awful.

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:40.477
And he'd just like, berate us.

00:04:40.477 --> 00:04:43.644
He was constantly pushing that button.

00:04:44.697 --> 00:04:48.548
And it wasn't, it never was like mean.

00:04:48.548 --> 00:04:51.200
Well, it was mean, but it was nice.

00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:53.268
(crowd laughing)

00:04:53.268 --> 00:04:55.867
It was always out of love,
because Jim just wanted

00:04:55.867 --> 00:04:59.115
to see us be better at
things, and he loved,

00:04:59.115 --> 00:05:02.424
I think your dad really loved
that competitive environment,

00:05:02.424 --> 00:05:06.507
and being a part of it,
and just experiencing it.

00:05:08.398 --> 00:05:11.315
(electronic music)

00:05:25.132 --> 00:05:28.438
The wave I rode, I think
I was eight years old.

00:05:28.438 --> 00:05:30.912
That wave stands out in
my head probably more

00:05:30.912 --> 00:05:33.491
than any wave in my life for some reason.

00:05:33.491 --> 00:05:37.370
It wasn't any sort of amazing wave, it was

00:05:37.370 --> 00:05:41.453
just a little wave, probably
waist or chest high.

00:05:42.443 --> 00:05:44.372
I took off on it, and then I just did,

00:05:44.372 --> 00:05:47.350
I did a bottom turn and I
did a little turn off the top

00:05:47.350 --> 00:05:48.983
and I just did that three different times,

00:05:48.983 --> 00:05:50.738
and when I finished the
third one I came down,

00:05:50.738 --> 00:05:52.364
I sort of straightened off on the wave,

00:05:52.364 --> 00:05:54.147
and I looked over, my
dad was smiling at me.

00:05:54.147 --> 00:05:57.761
And right at that moment, it
was such sheer joy, you know?

00:05:57.761 --> 00:06:00.496
Connecting with my dad,
but also finding the thing

00:06:00.496 --> 00:06:02.310
that I was becoming good at.

00:06:02.310 --> 00:06:04.004
I think, probably in that moment I knew

00:06:04.004 --> 00:06:07.267
that this is what I was
going to do forever.

00:06:07.267 --> 00:06:10.184
(electronic music)

00:06:24.945 --> 00:06:26.758
And I was inspired to go
travel around the world.

00:06:26.758 --> 00:06:28.176
I wanted to be able to make money

00:06:28.176 --> 00:06:31.641
and travel around the world
and surf all these great

00:06:31.641 --> 00:06:33.375
tropical places.

00:06:33.375 --> 00:06:38.190
At the end of it all, after
going all these places

00:06:38.190 --> 00:06:40.339
for so many years and
doing so many things,

00:06:40.339 --> 00:06:42.796
the real inspiration comes from just

00:06:42.796 --> 00:06:44.670
the pleasure of doing things.

00:06:44.670 --> 00:06:48.385
Just from, you know, it
doesn't have to be the best

00:06:48.385 --> 00:06:51.457
or biggest or most exciting
wave or journey or whatever.

00:06:51.457 --> 00:06:55.506
It still, it really comes
down to sharing with people.

00:06:55.506 --> 00:06:58.276
You know, having time with
your friends and your family

00:06:58.276 --> 00:07:00.790
and the people you love.

00:07:00.790 --> 00:07:03.707
(electronic music)

00:07:25.615 --> 00:07:27.694
Most of the things I
do are things I enjoy,

00:07:27.694 --> 00:07:31.172
and you know it kind of fulfills my life,

00:07:31.172 --> 00:07:34.163
and some things make me
busy and can distract me,

00:07:34.163 --> 00:07:35.746
so I got to, you know, make sure

00:07:35.746 --> 00:07:37.465
I don't do too many of those.

00:07:37.465 --> 00:07:40.578
You know, if you get too
focused on competition sometimes

00:07:40.578 --> 00:07:42.139
and you don't have
anything else happening,

00:07:42.139 --> 00:07:45.540
you get lopsided, you know,
and I've done that before.

00:07:45.540 --> 00:07:48.392
I don't really want to go
back in that direction.

00:07:48.392 --> 00:07:50.472
You got to structure things your own way.

00:07:50.472 --> 00:07:51.468
Take advice that's going to help you

00:07:51.468 --> 00:07:53.148
and structure it your own way.

00:07:53.148 --> 00:07:55.674
I feel like generally I
can maintain what I do

00:07:55.674 --> 00:07:58.424
and focus on the competition too.

00:08:00.260 --> 00:08:03.177
(electronic music)

00:08:19.676 --> 00:08:22.352
I've been really, you know, no excuses,

00:08:22.352 --> 00:08:25.508
I've put in 100 percent effort, you know,

00:08:25.508 --> 00:08:27.853
when I've surfed heats
the last couple years.

00:08:27.853 --> 00:08:29.898
But I have been, especially
in the last year,

00:08:29.898 --> 00:08:31.956
been very distracted by
a lot of other stuff.

00:08:31.956 --> 00:08:36.490
Just, I had many, many years
where I had all my energy

00:08:36.490 --> 00:08:38.415
strictly going in to trying
to improve my surfing,

00:08:38.415 --> 00:08:41.567
win heats, and that was my laser focus.

00:08:41.567 --> 00:08:44.255
And, you know, the last
couple of years have been

00:08:44.255 --> 00:08:46.098
a little bit more
business and side things.

00:08:46.098 --> 00:08:49.594
And also, you know, just a slight lack

00:08:49.594 --> 00:08:51.588
of the same amount of interest, you know,

00:08:51.588 --> 00:08:55.553
it's not as exciting after
all these years and stuff.

00:08:55.553 --> 00:08:58.016
I mean, I don't know
what you felt at the end

00:08:58.016 --> 00:08:59.897
of your career but, you
know, at some point you start

00:08:59.897 --> 00:09:02.831
kind of going, well I've
been here, I've done that,

00:09:02.831 --> 00:09:04.050
I've surfed against this guy.

00:09:04.050 --> 00:09:06.883
What's the new excitement in that?

00:09:08.563 --> 00:09:11.480
(electronic music)

00:09:23.789 --> 00:09:26.428
I'm super inspired to
get better at surfing.

00:09:26.428 --> 00:09:29.765
You know, if you're
not getting better than

00:09:29.765 --> 00:09:32.961
it's not fun anymore, that's my theory.

00:09:32.961 --> 00:09:34.361
If you're getting worse at something,

00:09:34.361 --> 00:09:35.919
you might as well find something else

00:09:35.919 --> 00:09:37.449
that you can get good at.

00:09:37.449 --> 00:09:39.857
I had an idea of how I
wanted to surf waves,

00:09:39.857 --> 00:09:41.417
and I wanted to be able to do that.

00:09:41.417 --> 00:09:44.820
And I didn't see really
anyone who surfed that way.

00:09:44.820 --> 00:09:47.365
You know, the way I thought.

00:09:47.365 --> 00:09:50.282
(electronic music)

00:10:16.229 --> 00:10:18.275
When you're forming an idea,
when you have a vision,

00:10:18.275 --> 00:10:21.900
whether it's music, or it's
a company, or a technology.

00:10:21.900 --> 00:10:25.685
You have people thinking
about it that are coming up,

00:10:25.685 --> 00:10:27.378
brainstorming all these great ideas

00:10:27.378 --> 00:10:28.728
and then it starts to get bigger,

00:10:28.728 --> 00:10:30.239
and then you need people who can reach out

00:10:30.239 --> 00:10:32.905
in those different branches
and grab the right people.

00:10:32.905 --> 00:10:35.394
And I think that's where
the success comes in.

00:10:35.394 --> 00:10:37.108
You got to have that like mind,

00:10:37.108 --> 00:10:39.242
but then you have to have different ideas

00:10:39.242 --> 00:10:41.482
and different viewpoints.

00:10:41.482 --> 00:10:45.363
I guess a measure of success is, you know,

00:10:45.363 --> 00:10:49.134
the end result, whatever comes in the end.

00:10:49.134 --> 00:10:52.051
(electronic music)

00:11:00.061 --> 00:11:02.836
I was in Fiji just a month after he died.

00:11:02.836 --> 00:11:04.134
And I was surfing in this event,

00:11:04.134 --> 00:11:07.563
the waves are absolutely perfect.

00:11:07.563 --> 00:11:10.651
And I just was sitting there
while I was surfing the heat

00:11:10.651 --> 00:11:12.212
and just started daydreaming about my dad,

00:11:12.212 --> 00:11:13.897
and I just went, gosh my
dad's never surfed one wave

00:11:13.897 --> 00:11:17.032
as good as any of these waves out here.

00:11:17.032 --> 00:11:19.948
And all the sudden this wave came,

00:11:19.948 --> 00:11:24.115
and it was uh, it was, I'm
not a religious person,

00:11:25.857 --> 00:11:27.572
I don't even know if I believe in God,

00:11:27.572 --> 00:11:30.288
but it was a gift from
God, it was like this pssh.

00:11:30.288 --> 00:11:32.441
(soft piano music)

00:11:32.441 --> 00:11:35.524
And I took off on it, for
people that don't know

00:11:35.524 --> 00:11:37.729
about surfing, the ultimate
thing is to get a barrel.

00:11:37.729 --> 00:11:39.054
Get inside the tube.

00:11:39.054 --> 00:11:41.728
And I think I pulled
into the barrel for about

00:11:41.728 --> 00:11:44.689
10, 12 seconds on this wave or something.

00:11:44.689 --> 00:11:49.432
And, it was right, I swear,
15 seconds after I had had

00:11:49.432 --> 00:11:52.021
this thought in my head
about, I wish my dad

00:11:52.021 --> 00:11:53.725
could catch one of these waves.

00:11:53.725 --> 00:11:54.985
And then that wave came.

00:11:54.985 --> 00:11:57.139
And I came out of the
wave, and I just like

00:11:57.139 --> 00:12:00.280
pointed at the sky, and it was like,

00:12:00.280 --> 00:12:02.947
I knew my dad was there with me.

00:12:03.960 --> 00:12:07.327
It was almost like that wave that I got

00:12:07.327 --> 00:12:10.410
when I was eight years old, you know?

00:12:12.079 --> 00:12:14.996
(electronic music)

